# bigo kata profile — the cost model of an algorithm-kata grading rubric.
#
# THIS FILE DELIBERATELY CONTRADICTS the curated cost table, and it wins over
# it (see callsummary.UseOverlay). The table states worst-case truth. This file
# states what a kata is graded on. Both are correct; they answer different
# questions. It applies ONLY under -kata.
#
# K-1  I/O is not work. Reading input, writing output and the scaffolding
#      around them lie outside the evaluated region. This is the rubric's own
#      rule, stated in every solution these entries were measured against:
#      "Чтение входящего массива, его обработка до поиска и вывод результата
#      не оценивается."
# K-2  An element operation costs unit. Comparing two records is O(1).
#
# Keys use costtable.FuncKey vocabulary. Add an entry only with a reason: an
# entry with no justification is a bound nobody has reasoned about.

# --- K-1: input scanning -------------------------------------------------
bufio.NewScanner O(1)
# Allocates a scanner. The reading it enables is input, not algorithm work.
bufio.NewWriter O(1)
# Mirror of NewScanner on the output side.
(*bufio.Scanner).Scan O(1)
# Advances to the next token of INPUT. The real cost is linear in the line
# read, and under K-1 the input is not part of what is graded.
(*bufio.Scanner).Text O(1)
# Returns the token Scan already read.
(*bufio.Scanner).Buffer O(1)
# Configures capacity before scanning; no traversal.
(*bufio.Writer).WriteString O(1)
# Emits a result. Output is not graded.
(*bufio.Writer).Flush O(1)
# Emits buffered output.

# --- K-1: parsing the input text ----------------------------------------
strconv.Atoi O(1)
# Curated linear in the string, correctly. That string is an INPUT token, so
# under K-1 its length is not a size the algorithm is graded on.
strings.Split O(1)
# Same reason: splitting an input line is input handling.
strings.Fields O(1)
# The other common way the same input line gets split.

# --- K-2: element operations --------------------------------------------
cmp.Compare O(1)
# One comparison of two elements is one element operation.
strings.Compare O(1)
# Curated linear in the operand, correctly. Under K-2 a comparison inside a
# comparator is one element operation — which is what these solutions assert
# by hand: "сравнение участников - это O(1)".

# --- convention ----------------------------------------------------------
math/rand.Intn O(1)
# bigo deliberately refuses this family: Int31n rejects and retries, so there
# is an EXPECTED iteration count and no worst-case one. Randomized pivot
# selection is conventionally O(1). The expected-versus-worst-case split is a
# separate feature and is not smuggled in here.
